// The globals the Rust side talks to. `Chrome` carries activity and the idle
// clock, `Flash` the brief feedback and notices, `Tip` the hover label —
// each a global because what raises it is buried inside components that
// would otherwise need a callback threaded through every call site.

/// Chrome visibility. Every interactive element reports activity here; the
/// Rust side owns the idle clock and pushes `idle` back.
///
/// A global rather than a callback on MainWindow because activity comes from
/// deep inside reusable components (buttons, menu rows), and threading a
/// callback through every one of them would be noise at each call site.
export global Chrome {
    /// Reports activity to the Rust side, which owns the idle clock.
    callback activity();
    /// Whether the keyboard's ring should be drawn at all.
    ///
    /// Off until the keyboard is actually steering: a panel opened with a
    /// key, an arrow, Tab, or Enter. A ring drawn over a panel someone
    /// reached for with the mouse is a second highlight competing with the
    /// row that is actually playing, and it points at a row the hand is not
    /// on. It goes away again the moment a mouse button goes down.
    in-out property <bool> focus-visible: false;
    /// Whether nothing has happened for a while.
    ///
    /// Cleared here rather than by the timer that sets it. The timer polls
    /// only a few times a second, and waiting for it left the cursor and
    /// chrome hidden for a visible moment after the mouse moved - the
    /// pointer would blink out just as the interface came back.
    in-out property <bool> idle: false;
    /// Bumped by every key and pointer event — input, as distinct from the
    /// activity a film ending also raises. A notice waits for the next one.
    in-out property <int> input-seq: 0;

    /// Call on any activity. Wakes the interface immediately and resets the
    /// clock behind it.
    public function bump() {
        Chrome.idle = false;
        Chrome.activity();
    }

    /// Ask for the pointer to leave the screen, or to come back.
    ///
    /// A callback rather than a property the Rust side polls, because the two
    /// directions are not equally urgent. Hiding can wait — it happens three
    /// seconds after the hand stopped, and a fifth of a second either way is
    /// not a thing anyone can see. Coming back cannot: the pointer has to be
    /// under the hand the instant it moves, and a clock ticking five times a
    /// second would leave it missing after the chrome was already up.
    callback hide-pointer(hide: bool);

    /// Pointer activity, and whether a button went down with it. The same
    /// wake-up as `bump`, plus the ring standing down: the hand has taken
    /// over, and two cursors on one panel is one too many.
    public function pointed(down: bool) {
        Chrome.bump();
        Chrome.input-seq += 1;
        if (down) {
            Chrome.focus-visible = false;
        }
    }

    /// The same, from a row the keyboard can also stand on.
    ///
    /// A pointer moving over a row the ring is not on stands the ring down.
    /// Otherwise a hand resting in a panel lights one row while the keys light
    /// another, and the two washes say nothing about which one Enter presses.
    /// Only movement counts: a pointer that is merely still there raises no
    /// events, so arrowing past it keeps the ring.
    public function pointed-at(down: bool, moved: bool, focused: bool) {
        Chrome.pointed(down);
        if (moved && !focused) {
            Chrome.focus-visible = false;
        }
    }
}

/// Brief feedback for an action whose effect is otherwise off screen.
///
/// A global for the same reason `Chrome` is one: it is raised from the key
/// handlers, which are functions rather than elements, and threading a
/// callback down to each of them would be noise at every call site.
///
/// Only the actions the bar cannot speak for are here. Mute, volume and
/// panscan all move a control that is already on screen — a key press wakes
/// the chrome — and saying it twice is not saying it better. What is left is
/// what nothing else shows: which way a seek went, that the file changed,
/// what the subtitle delay landed on, and whether subtitles are on at all,
/// which the subtitles button stopped saying when it stopped being a switch.
export global Flash {
    // Which glyph, as an index into the window's own list: `@image-url`
    // resolves at compile time, so the images cannot live here. Named rather
    // than numbered at the call sites, since the order of that list is
    // invisible from them.
    out property <int> seek-back: 0;
    out property <int> seek-forward: 1;
    out property <int> previous: 2;
    out property <int> next: 3;
    out property <int> restart: 4;
    out property <int> delay: 5;

    in-out property <int> icon: 0;
    /// A sentence, when the player has something to say rather than something
    /// to acknowledge. Empty for an ordinary action flash.
    ///
    /// A notice shows the capsule alone, with no ring: the icon set has no
    /// glyph for a failure, and a Unicode character standing in for one would
    /// be the only borrowed mark in the interface.
    in-out property <string> message: "";
    /// Where across the picture it appears, 0..1. A seek is thrown towards
    /// the side it seeks to; everything else sits in the middle.
    in-out property <float> at: 0.5;
    /// Bumped on every raise, so the window can restart the dwell even when
    /// the same action repeats — which is the common case, since these are
    /// keys people hold.
    in-out property <int> seq: 0;
    /// A figure to show on its own, with no ring: 1 the speed, 2 the chapter,
    /// 3 the subtitle track, 4 the audio track. The icon set has no glyph for
    /// any of them, and the figure is the whole answer anyway. 0 for none.
    in-out property <int> figure: 0;

    public function show-figure(kind: int) {
        Flash.figure = kind;
        Flash.message = "";
        Flash.at = 0.5;
        Flash.seq += 1;
    }

    public function show(icon: int, at: float) {
        Flash.icon = icon;
        Flash.figure = 0;
        Flash.message = "";
        Flash.at = at;
        Flash.seq += 1;
    }

    /// Say something. Used where the alternative was a line on a console that
    /// a packaged build does not have — a file that will not play, a folder
    /// with nothing in it, sound coming back after a device disappeared.
    public function notice(text: string) {
        Flash.message = text;
        Flash.figure = 0;
        Flash.at = 0.5;
        Flash.seq += 1;
    }
}

/// What a control is called, and which key does the same thing.
///
/// The bar is thirteen drawn marks and no words. They are the project's own
/// icons and the set is the better for having no labels stamped under it, but
/// a mark that is never explained anywhere teaches nobody: the only account
/// of what they do lived on a panel you had to know to open in order to find
/// out what the button that opens it does.
///
/// One surface for the whole bar rather than one per button. Only ever one
/// tip is up, and thirteen labels would be thirteen more rects published to
/// the renderer for a thing that is never in two places at once.
///
/// The key rides along because that is the part worth learning. A tooltip
/// that only repeats the glyph is a label; one that names the key is the
/// reference card arriving where the question was asked.
export global Tip {
    in-out property <string> label;
    in-out property <string> keys;
    /// Centre of the control it belongs to, in window coordinates.
    in-out property <length> cx;
    /// Bumped on every arrival and departure, so the window can watch one
    /// property rather than three - and so a control can tell whether the
    /// tip is still the one it put up.
    in-out property <int> seq;

    /// Puts a tip up and returns the claim on it, for `release`.
    public function point(label: string, keys: string, cx: length) -> int {
        Tip.label = label;
        Tip.keys = keys;
        Tip.cx = cx;
        Tip.seq += 1;
        return Tip.seq;
    }

    /// Takes the tip down, but only if `claim` is still the one up.
    ///
    /// Ownership was once decided by position: a control cleared the tip if
    /// it was centred where that control is. Anything that moves a control
    /// while its tip is up broke that - fullscreen above all, which moves the
    /// very button that was pressed - and the tip then stayed up for good,
    /// since nothing else would ever claim it.
    public function release(claim: int) {
        if (Tip.seq == claim) {
            Tip.clear();
        }
    }

    public function clear() {
        Tip.label = "";
        Tip.seq += 1;
    }
}
